{"id": "sdqa0100", "pool": "sdqa", "tier": "always", "audio_s": 4.4, "n_chunks": 5, "n_concurrent": 5, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.114, "eot_read_ms": 20, "reference_answer": "exerts control by switching heating or cooling devices on or off, or by regulating the flow of a heat transfer fluid as needed, to maintain the correct temperature", "query": "How do thermostats work?", "transcript": "How do thermostats work?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "Thermostats work by measuring the temperature of a space and switching heating or cooling equipment on or off to keep it near a set temperature. You set a target temperature, like 72°F, and the thermostat uses a sensor to check the current temperature. If it's too cold, it turns the heater on; if it's too warm, it signals the air conditioner to kick in. Once the room reaches your desired range, it turns the system off. Many thermostats have a small buffer zone so the system doesn't cycle on and off constantly with tiny temperature changes. Old ones used mechanical parts like bimetallic strips, while modern digital versions use sensors, microprocessors, and sometimes even Wi-Fi for extra features.", "expert_answer": "Thermostats work by measuring the temperature of a space and switching heating or cooling equipment on or off to keep it near a set temperature.\n\n- **You set a target temperature** (the “setpoint”), such as 72°F / 22°C.\n- **A sensor measures the current temperature** in the room.\n- **The thermostat compares** the measured temperature to the setpoint.\n- If the room is **too cold**, it signals the **heater** to turn on.\n- If the room is **too warm**, it signals the **air conditioner** to turn on.\n- When the room reaches the desired range, it turns the system off.\n\nMost thermostats use a small temperature buffer called a **deadband** or **hysteresis**, so the system does not rapidly turn on and off every time the temperature changes by a tiny amount.\n\nOlder mechanical thermostats often used **bimetallic strips** or mercury switches. Modern digital and smart thermostats use **electronic temperature sensors**, microprocessors, schedules, and sometimes Wi‑Fi, motion detection, and learning algorithms to improve comfort and efficiency.", "expert_latency_s": 5.6, "stall_ms": 22, "relay_ms": 3617}
